 3d it's just really really  special yeah Scott the director talks  about how he purposefully chose to sort  of go from that she went to the other  instead of really doing the whole thing  and yeah yeah can you talk about what  kind of impact well it's in the end it's  a creative decision you know we're not  doing it just because we can do it we're  just doing it because it brings  something special to the film and I  think in that sense we've been very  cautious about the moments where we  wanted to stay into Thoreau and the  moments when we actually go to 1.9  there's a big example of that is there's  a scene at the beginning of a film which  is called we call the magic mystery tour  where dr. strange is pushed into all  these dimensions by the ancient one and  that's the you know  one of the first time in the film where  we actually go to 1.9 and the experience  is very different because you you're  travelling through all these space and  all these dimensions and it's it's it  just you know it makes it bigger and  more immersive definitely were there any  unique or innovative techniques that you  sort of had to create or discover while  working on this film well I mean there's  there's a lot of things that we've been  you know doing R&amp;D on on the film on  many levels like from a creative point  of view doing some R&amp;D on how to praying  the world of optical illusion and usher  and kaleidoscopic stuff to the screen  and making sense of it all the  distortion of the buildings in the  cities and all that stuff to a huge  scale so definitely there's a creative  side of it there's also a very technical  side of it in terms of the way we're  shooting stuff the use of special rigs  and and motion control cameras and in a  in a in a way that was allowing us to  keep the action going and and you know  all the things that we've been doing for  the film there's lots of aspects in  terms of R&amp;D that you know I'm not going  to go in details on but there's there's  