y complex  and you know it takes a lot of planning  and working out the solutions to shoot  these things and then accomplish them in  post so all of the sequences at their  own little challenge and I would say  every shot had their own challenge and  you know we're bringing a new magic to  the world as well so it's all very  changing in many different ways is there  any particular scene or shot or location  or sequence that you're especially proud  of and excited for people to experience  well they're all my babies so it's  difficult to choose but I would say I  like Hong Kong because it's kind of  special you know it's good that  backwards destruction feel which i think  is actually a new thing in a blockbuster  movie usually in you know in this kind  of movies we end up destroying half of  the world right and now if you look at  it at the end of Doctor Strange we've  not destroyed anything we've actually  rebuilt Hong Kong it was destroyed and  we're going backwards and so we're  taking the whole idea of a blockbuster  film but putting it upside down which i  think is pretty clever actually so I  think I'm pretty proud of that you know  of the  idea and the way we've done it on screen  yeah I really enjoyed that aspect of  Doctor Strange - it sort of subverts  though yeah yeah yeah we're trying we're  trying to say okay well we're not gonna  finish like the other guys we're  destroying everything we're just gonna  go backwards we're gonna rebuild the  entire thing and at the end everything's  back to normal  and I think that was a fun twist on the  old blockbuster idea and just to go back  to IMAX for a second um what do you  think that extra top and bottom ad and  what was the process of going in and and  and making sure that it was you know  worth going to see and yeah well there's  lots of you know when you work into  furrow there's lots of work we don't do  up-and-down to be honest and here it was  like actually more work to do that but  every time we are going into sequences  that required that extra detail and  extra information at the top and bottom  we're thinking okay so what are we  putting in there and that is worthwhile  putting because you know it just it's an  interesting format that we can play with  so every time we had a sequence who are  looking at okay so we can put that oh  we're going to put that thing up there  so that the guys from IMAX can see it  only these guys can see it so it was  like finding some little clever things  or reframing shots so that we would see  more at the top and the bottom and some  more interesting information and do you  have any behind the scenes things that  we should look for when watching the  movie like is there anything that you  want to like you know hint that that we  should be on the lookout oh I don't know  well I mean you know it's every show  there's a story obviously some funny  stories some some difficult stories some  like oh my god what are we gonna do that  stories but there's too many of them for  me to get into it's yeah it's just I  don't know what you say really so do you  have any words of advice for people who  are interests  and diving in and working in VFX right  well I mean if they're interested they  should really look into trying to find  out how we do things you know there's  lots of publications and things online  where you can look at it I think the  main thing for me if you want to work in  visual effects you have to learn to be a  storyteller as well because I think it's  nowadays with visual effects we can do  pretty much everything you know  depending on the budget and the time you  have obviously but I think what makes  the difference is when you make  something that tells a story so as a  visual effects artist I think you have  to be very cautious about that and very  aware of what your your work is doing  and what your work is bringing to the  story so that's my advice so now in  terms of getting into the craft and  getting into the work yo